    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2e9937fa-f0e1-536b-a2ef-11a23cc0ffb9",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+10,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1e90ae91-984d-5e43-8fa8-9535f4d4397f",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
