      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+11,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$",
