    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1f97358a-5ba9-5577-bd31-6810de94a6f6",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=8\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
